ng time doesn’t make it *true*. It just makes it *old*. And plenty of old, long-repeated ideas have turned out to be deeply unfair—about race, about class, about what women can or can’t do. We don’t accept “that’s just how they are” when it comes to other groups, because we recognize that it’s a way of shutting down accountability. The same applies here. Men are not pre-programmed to be aggressive jerks when they don’t get their way. They are *taught*, often from toddlerhood, that their anger is powerful and that others—especially women—will accommodate it. That’s not nature; that’s nurture, repeated so consistently that it can feel indistinguishable from nature. The danger in fully accepting “boys will be boys” as an unchangeable rule is that it doesn’t just describe the past; it shapes your future. It can make you lower your standards, excuse behavior that genuinely hurts you, or believe that hoping for a man who is gentle, accountable, and respectful is like believing in fairy tales. And your grandmother and great-grandmother probably needed that belief to survive their circumstances—but you don’t have to live under the same limits. You don’t have to pass that resignation on. I’m not asking you to suddenly trust every man, or to forget the hurt you’ve seen with your own eyes. I’m suggesting that you can hold two truths at once: that many men *do* behave the way you’ve witnessed, and that this behavior is a learned, excusable failing—not a fixed male essence. Recognizing that difference gives you power. It means you can say, “I won’t accept this, not because I’m naive, but because I know it’s not okay and it *can* be different.” And it means your cousin can look at her brother and say, “I see you acting like a brute, and I’m not going to just shrug my shoulders and say that’s how boys are—because I expect more from you.” Your grandmother wasn’t wrong to see the pattern; she was likely powerless to change it. You might be in a different position. You don’t have to 