20:37:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really thoughtful question, and I’m glad you asked it. When you’re living with a painful pattern and then stack on top of it the common belief that testosterone equals aggression, or that animal males are all domineering, the “biology is destiny” argument can feel airtight. And it’s true that biology does play some role in our makeup—but the way that role gets overstated is often used, deliberately or not, to let harmful behavior off the hook. Let’s unpack it carefully. First, testosterone and aggression in humans. Yes, testosterone can be associated with *some forms* of competitive, status-seeking behavior or physical drive. But research has repeatedly shown that the link is nowhere near the simple “more T = more aggression” formula people assume. Many studies find only a weak correlation, and some find none at all. Context and social learning actually shape testosterone levels—a man’s testosterone can rise before a competitive game but drop if he loses, for instance. More importantly, testosterone doesn’t force specific behaviors; it lowers thresholds for certain impulses, but humans have an enormous cerebral cortex that lets us decide what to do with those impulses. The vast majority of men with high testosterone are not running around being aggressive brutes. If testosterone automatically made someone a jerk, then every hyped-up teenage boy would be a lost cause, and gentle men would have to have hormone deficiencies—neither of which is true. Second, the animal comparison. It’s tempting to point to other species and say, “See, males are naturally aggressive everywhere.” But this is a massive oversimplification. In many species, females are the dominant or more aggressive ones—hyenas, certain birds, some primates. Among our closest relatives, bonobos, aggression is relatively low, and females hold significant social power; they use sexual behavior and bonding rather than brute force to resolve tensions. Chimpanzees, by contrast, do have male-dominated hierarchies with violence—but even there, the expression of aggression is heavily influenced by environment and social group, not just an automatic reflex. What we project onto “nature” is often our own cultural lens. Nature contains enormous diversity of behavioral patterns, not one single script. Most crucially, humans have something no other animal has: complex culture, morality, and self-regulation. We can and do unlearn harmful behaviors all the time. If biology were an inescapable cage, we’d never have stopped ourselves from any action driven by hunger, fear, or lust. Yet we manage not to steal food even when starving, not to hit everyone who angers us, and to stick to traffic laws. The same capacity applies to how men treat others. When a man turns into an aggressive jerk “the second things don’t go his way,” that’s not a hormonal lightning strike—it’s a learned habit of using anger to regain control, one that often got him what he wanted in the past. It can be unlearned, just like any other bad habit, if there’s motivation and accountability. The danger of the “it’s just biology” argument is that it frames the behavior as natural and therefore acceptable—which then puts the burden back on the person receiving it to endure it gracefully. That’s the same quiet resignation your grandma and her mom lived with. But acknowledging that biology contributes an impulse does not mean excusing the action. A dog may have a natural instinct to chase a squirrel, but we still expect a trained dog to obey commands and not drag its owner into traffic. We expect humans to have even more developed self-control. So what does this mean for your cousin, or for you, in real life? It means she—and you—are allowed to hold men to a standard higher than their raw impulses. It’s not unreasonable to expect a brother, a father, a coworker, or a partner to manage his frustration without turning into a “brute.” If he fails, that’s a failure of his effort and possibly his environment, not an unavoidable expression of his chromosomes. I know this can feel like cold comfort when you keep running into the same disappointing reality. But knowing that the biology is not destiny gives you back your voice. You don’t have to accept bad behavior as an act of nature. You can call it what it is—a choice pattern, however deeply ingrained—and decide that you deserve to be around people who are working to choose better.
